ZX-UX C48 SDK
SANYALnet Labs
C48 RECURSION AND MULTITASK DISPLAY RUNBOOK
Revision 1.1
Date: 2026-09-27

1. PURPOSE

This runbook freezes the design and later implementation procedure for two
tracked C48 examples:

  usr/src/examples/hanoi.c
  usr/bin/examples/hanoi.c48b

  usr/src/examples/queens8.c
  usr/bin/examples/queens8.c48b

The immediate SDK goal is to compile and prove each example independently.

The larger ZX-UX goal is to run both examples as separate tasks on one
256x192 Spectrum display without either task writing into the other task's
half of the screen or user-defined graphics allocation.

The secondary language goal is to provide strong, visible proof that C48
recursive calls, local frame state, return paths, and repeated unwind/re-entry
work correctly.

This document is design and execution procedure only. It does not contain the
program implementations.

2. NON-NEGOTIABLE DISPLAY CONTRACT

The physical display is 256x192 pixels.

The C48 text surface is 64 columns by 24 rows. Each text column is 4 pixels
wide and each text row is 8 pixels high.

The user-defined graphics surface is 32 columns by 24 rows. Each graphics cell
is 8x8 pixels.

Resource ownership is exact:

  HANOI
    Pixels:              x=0..127
    Human text columns:  1..32
    C48 text columns:    0..31
    Graphics columns:    0..15
    UDG slots:           0..15

  8-QUEENS
    Pixels:              x=128..255
    Human text columns:  33..64
    C48 text columns:    32..63
    Graphics columns:    16..31
    UDG slots:           16..31

The x=128 boundary is also an 8-pixel attribute-cell boundary. Therefore text,
bitmap graphics, and colour attributes can all be partitioned without a cell
straddling both programs.

Neither program may call cls() at any point.

Neither program may call border().

Neither program may define, clear, or draw a UDG slot owned by the other
program.

Neither program may print, erase, or draw outside its owned columns.

Each program must clear only its own half by local row repainting.

Every draw helper must receive or derive an owned coordinate and reject an
out-of-range coordinate before the host drawing call is made.

3. SHARED-SCREEN MULTITASKING CONTRACT

The examples are deliberately independent processes. They do not communicate
with each other and do not require a shared memory protocol.

Their only shared hardware-facing resources are the screen and any ZX-UX
graphics state underlying the C48 host calls.

The split UDG allocation prevents direct UDG definition collisions when the
UDG table is shared.

Each visible drawing operation must explicitly establish the required ink,
paper, brightness, inverse, and over state before drawing. The concurrent ZX-UX
test is expected to expose any failure by the operating system to preserve
task-local graphics state across scheduling points.

A task switch during recursive execution is intentional. Visible steps include
yield or sleep scheduling points while parent recursive frames are still live.

The programs never depend on the other task's speed, state, start order, or
completion order.

The SDK proves each program independently. Side-by-side scheduling is a
ZX-UX integration proof and is not to be faked inside one SDK process.

4. COMMON EXECUTION MODES

Each program has two timing modes using the same algorithm and the same draw
sequence.

NORMAL MODE
  Default when launched by a human.
  Uses visible delays at 50-Hz tick granularity.
  Intended to make recursion, motion, placement, and backtracking easy to
  follow.

FAST VERIFICATION MODE
  Selected by a command-line argument reserved for verification.
  Performs the complete recursion and the same state transitions.
  Removes human pacing delays.
  Still reaches scheduling/presentation points so the full execution path is
  exercised.
  Returns the same success or failure status as normal mode.

Fast mode exists only to keep regression verification practical. It may not
skip moves, candidate tests, recursive calls, invariant checks, or final-state
validation.

5. TOWERS OF HANOI: FROZEN DESIGN

5.1 Workload

Use seven discs.

The correct move count is:

  2^7 - 1 = 127 moves

The recursive depth is seven Hanoi frames.

The initial state is all seven discs on pole A, largest at the bottom.

The target state is all seven discs on pole C, largest at the bottom.

Pole B is the spare pole.

5.2 Screen geometry

Hanoi owns the complete left 128x192 half.

Suggested fixed layout:

  Row 0:  TOWERS OF HANOI
  Row 1:  MOVE nnn/127   DEP d/7
  Row 2:  DISC n   A->C style current-move text
  Rows 3-5:
          lift and horizontal travel zone
  Rows 6-16:
          pole and stack zone
  Row 17:
          common base
  Row 18:
          A / B / C pole labels
  Row 19:
          current action
  Row 20:
          frame-guard status
  Row 21:
          spare status line
  Row 22:
          maximum depth observed
  Row 23:
          SEARCHING, SOLVED, PASS, or FAIL

Pole graphics-cell centres are:

  A: graphics column 2, pixel centre near x=20
  B: graphics column 7, pixel centre near x=60
  C: graphics column 12, pixel centre near x=100

This leaves visible separation between the widest discs on adjacent poles.

The pole runs vertically through rows 6..16.

Seven stacked discs occupy rows 10..16 at maximum height.

The base occupies row 17.

The raised travel path uses row 4.

5.3 Disc geometry

Disc widths are:

  Disc 1:  8 pixels
  Disc 2: 12 pixels
  Disc 3: 16 pixels
  Disc 4: 20 pixels
  Disc 5: 24 pixels
  Disc 6: 28 pixels
  Disc 7: 32 pixels

Disc 1 is smallest. Disc 7 is largest.

The widths increase in four-pixel steps after the smallest disc. Cap patterns
permit endpoints inside 8-pixel cells while keeping the disc centred on a pole
cell.

Disc shape is a compact rounded bar with a slightly narrower top and bottom
scan line. Width, not colour, is the authoritative size cue.

Use bright non-black Spectrum colours for the seven disc identities. The pole
and base use a neutral high-contrast colour on black paper.

5.4 Hanoi UDG allocation

Slots 0..15 belong only to Hanoi.

Freeze the pattern roles as:

  0   blank/background
  1   vertical pole
  2   horizontal base
  3   pole/base junction
  4   left rounded cap, 2-pixel extent
  5   left rounded cap, 4-pixel extent
  6   left rounded cap, 6-pixel extent
  7   left rounded cap, 8-pixel extent
  8   full disc body
  9   right rounded cap, 8-pixel extent
  10  right rounded cap, 6-pixel extent
  11  right rounded cap, 4-pixel extent
  12  right rounded cap, 2-pixel extent
  13  complete 8-pixel smallest disc
  14  reserved
  15  reserved

The cap set is sufficient to render all seven frozen widths without borrowing a
slot from the right-side program.

5.5 Hanoi model state

Keep the logical model separate from the display.

Required state:

  pole_count[3]
  pole_disc[3][7]
  move_count
  current_disc
  current_source
  current_target
  current_depth
  maximum_depth
  invariant_failures
  frame_guard_failures

The display is always derived from the logical model. Display pixels are never
used as the source of truth for legal-move decisions.

5.6 Hanoi recursive algorithm

The recursive operation is the conventional form:

  solve(n, source, spare, target, depth)

For n greater than zero:

  1. Establish a local frame guard derived from n, source, spare, target,
     and depth.
  2. Update current depth and maximum depth.
  3. Recursively solve n-1 from source to spare using target as temporary.
  4. After return, verify the local frame guard is unchanged.
  5. Validate and animate exactly one legal move from source to target.
  6. Recursively solve n-1 from spare to target using source as temporary.
  7. After return, verify the local frame guard again.
  8. Return to the caller.

The explicit frame guard is part of the recursion proof. A frame-guard failure
is fatal and must prevent PASS.

5.7 Hanoi move validation

Before every move:

  source pole must not be empty
  moving disc must be the top source disc
  target must be empty or have a larger top disc
  moving disc number must be 1..7

After every move:

  every pole must remain strictly largest-to-smallest from bottom to top
  each disc 1..7 must exist exactly once
  total disc count must remain seven
  move_count must advance by exactly one

A failure sets the program failure state and terminates with a non-zero status.

5.8 Hanoi animation

A legal move is displayed as:

  LIFT
    Raise the moving disc one 8-pixel row at a time from the source stack to
    travel row 4.

  TRAVEL
    Move the disc horizontally one graphics column at a time toward the target
    pole.

  DROP
    Lower it one row at a time to the correct target stack height.

  SETTLE
    Repaint the source and target stack regions from the logical model, update
    counters, and pause briefly.

No animation step writes outside graphics columns 0..15.

Normal-mode pacing target:

  moving step:    2 ticks
  settled pause:  4 ticks

One tick is 1/50 second in the SDK host runtime.

A major move state also reaches a yield point. The intent is to permit task
switching while recursive callers remain live.

5.9 Hanoi completion proof

PASS requires all of the following:

  move_count == 127
  pole A empty
  pole B empty
  pole C contains seven discs
  pole C order is legal
  each disc exists exactly once
  maximum_depth == 7
  invariant_failures == 0
  frame_guard_failures == 0

The final left-half display contains:

  SOLVED
  127/127 MOVES
  MAX DEP 7
  FRAME GUARD OK
  PASS

Normal mode holds the final frame long enough to read it and then exits with
status zero.

Fast verification mode reaches the same final state without the human hold.

6. 8-QUEENS: FROZEN DESIGN

6.1 Workload

Solve the classic eight-queens placement problem.

The search is deterministic:

  rows are processed top to bottom
  candidate columns are tested left to right

Stop at the first valid complete solution.

With zero-based columns, the frozen expected first solution is:

  [0, 4, 7, 5, 2, 6, 1, 3]

With human columns A..H, the same row-by-row solution is:

  A, E, H, F, C, G, B, D

The recursive depth is eight placement frames.

6.2 Screen geometry

8-Queens owns the complete right 128x192 half.

Suggested fixed layout:

  Row 0:  8 QUEENS - RECURSIVE SEARCH
  Row 1:  TRY F6   DEP 6/8 style live text
  Row 2:  A   B   C   D   E   F   G   H
  Rows 3-18:
          128x128 chessboard
  Row 19:
          current row and candidate
  Row 20:
          placed count and backtrack count
  Row 21:
          frame-guard status
  Row 22:
          maximum depth observed
  Row 23:
          SEARCHING, SOLVED, PASS, or FAIL

The chessboard is exactly eight 16x16-pixel squares across and eight squares
high.

Each square is exactly 2x2 graphics cells.

The board therefore occupies graphics columns 16..31 and rows 3..18 with no
spill into the left half.

6.3 Board and queen appearance

Use alternating black and blue paper for the board.

A queen is a true 16x16 silhouette made from four UDG cells. The silhouette
has three crown points, a narrow neck, a wider body, and a flared base.

Three queen visual states exist:

  CANDIDATE
    outline queen, bright cyan

  COMMITTED
    solid queen, bright white

  BACKTRACK
    removal-state queen, bright red

The board square paper colour is restored before any queen state is drawn.
Erasing a queen means repainting all four cells of that square with the blank
board pattern and the correct square paper colour.

6.4 8-Queens UDG allocation

Slots 16..31 belong only to 8-Queens.

Freeze the pattern roles as:

  16..19  solid 2x2 queen
  20..23  outline 2x2 candidate queen
  24..27  backtrack 2x2 queen
  28      blank board-cell pattern
  29      conflict marker
  30      solved marker
  31      reserved

The three 2x2 queen sets are arranged so udg_draw_2x2 can use bases 16, 20,
and 24 directly.

The blank board cell is drawn with individual udg_draw calls so slots 29..31
remain available for their own roles.

6.5 8-Queens model state

Required state:

  queen_col[8]
  col_used[8]
  diag_down_used[15]
  diag_up_used[15]
  current_row
  current_col
  placed_count
  candidate_tests
  backtrack_count
  current_depth
  maximum_depth
  invariant_failures
  frame_guard_failures

queen_col[row] is -1 for an empty row or 0..7 for a committed queen.

The model is authoritative. The board pixels are presentation only.

6.6 8-Queens recursive algorithm

The recursive operation is:

  place(row, depth)

For each candidate column 0..7:

  1. Establish a local frame guard from row, depth, and the loop state.
  2. Show the candidate queen.
  3. Increment candidate_tests exactly once.
  4. Test column and both diagonals.
  5. If unsafe, erase the candidate and continue.
  6. If safe, commit the queen and mark all three occupancy sets.
  7. Increment placed_count.
  8. If row is seven, validate the complete board and report success.
  9. Otherwise recursively place row+1.
  10. After recursive return, verify the caller's frame guard and loop state.
  11. If the child succeeded, propagate success without modifying the solution.
  12. If the child failed, show the backtrack state, remove the queen, clear
      all three occupancy marks, decrement placed_count, increment
      backtrack_count, and continue the caller's next column.

A recursive call may never derive legality from the drawn board.

6.7 Diagonal indexing

Use fixed ranges that never require dynamic storage:

  column index:
    col = 0..7

  descending diagonal:
    row - col + 7 = 0..14

  ascending diagonal:
    row + col = 0..14

All indexes must be range-checked during development verification.

6.8 8-Queens frame-state proof

The loop variable in each recursive caller must survive the child call so the
caller can continue at the next candidate after a backtrack.

In addition to that natural test, each recursive frame keeps a small local
guard value.

After child return:

  guard must match its original value
  row must still identify the caller's row
  caller loop state must still identify the committed candidate

Any mismatch is fatal and prevents PASS.

6.9 8-Queens animation

Every candidate is visible.

Normal-mode pacing target:

  rejected candidate outline:  1 tick
  accepted placement pause:    4 ticks
  backtrack display pause:     4 ticks
  final solved hold:           100 ticks

A major placement or removal state also reaches a yield point.

This keeps the complete deterministic first-solution search visible while
remaining in roughly the same human-scale running window as the seven-disc
Hanoi demonstration.

Fast verification mode removes the waits but performs every candidate test,
placement, removal, recursive call, and frame-guard check.

6.10 8-Queens completion proof

PASS requires all of the following:

  exactly eight queens are committed
  every row contains one queen
  every column contains one queen
  no descending diagonal is shared
  no ascending diagonal is shared
  queen_col matches the frozen first-solution vector
  placed_count == 8
  maximum_depth == 8
  invariant_failures == 0
  frame_guard_failures == 0

The final right-half display contains:

  SOLUTION FOUND
  8/8 QUEENS
  MAX DEP 8
  FRAME GUARD OK
  PASS

Normal mode holds the final frame long enough to read it and then exits with
status zero.

Fast verification mode reaches the same final state without the human hold.

7. SIDE-BY-SIDE EXPECTED APPEARANCE

The visual composition is intentionally balanced.

LEFT HALF
  Dark field.
  Large, simple geometric pole-and-disc motion.
  Three fixed poles.
  Motion concentrated vertically and horizontally.
  Live move and recursion counters.
  Final complete tower at C.

RIGHT HALF
  Dense 8x8 chessboard.
  Candidate flicker and visible backtracking.
  Large 16x16 queen silhouettes.
  Live depth and backtrack counters.
  Final valid eight-queen arrangement.

The centre boundary at x=128 remains visually clean. No separator line is
required because the two full-height regions and different visual structures
already create a strong division. A separator may only be added if each side
draws its own edge pixel without crossing ownership.

8. SOURCE IMPLEMENTATION PROCEDURE

Implementation is to proceed in this order.

PHASE 1 - HANOI LOGIC WITHOUT GRAPHICS

  Create usr/src/examples/hanoi.c.
  Build the logical three-pole model.
  Implement seven-disc recursion.
  Implement frame guards.
  Implement legal-move and final-state validation.
  Run fast mode with no decorative drawing.
  Prove exactly 127 moves and depth seven.

Do not proceed until the logic terminates with status zero.

PHASE 2 - HANOI OWNED-HALF DRAWING

  Add UDG definitions 0..15 only.
  Add left-half clear helpers.
  Add pole, base, disc, and status rendering.
  Add lift/travel/drop animation.
  Add normal pacing.
  Re-run full fast verification.
  Confirm no draw coordinate exceeds the left-half contract.

PHASE 3 - 8-QUEENS LOGIC WITHOUT GRAPHICS

  Create usr/src/examples/queens8.c.
  Implement deterministic row-first recursion.
  Implement column and diagonal occupancy tables.
  Implement frame guards.
  Validate the frozen first solution.
  Prove depth eight and final board legality.

Do not proceed until the logic terminates with status zero.

PHASE 4 - 8-QUEENS OWNED-HALF DRAWING

  Add UDG definitions 16..31 only.
  Add right-half clear helpers.
  Add board and queen rendering.
  Add candidate and backtrack display states.
  Add normal pacing.
  Re-run full fast verification.
  Confirm no draw coordinate enters the left half.

PHASE 5 - TRACKED COMPILED CORPUS

  Compile:
    usr/src/examples/hanoi.c
    usr/src/examples/queens8.c

  Track:
    usr/bin/examples/hanoi.c48b
    usr/bin/examples/queens8.c48b

  Recompile both from clean source and confirm the tracked compiled artifacts
  are deterministic.

PHASE 6 - SDK INDIVIDUAL PROOF

  Run each tracked compiled artifact independently in fast verification mode.

  Require:
    clean process exit
    zero status
    complete recursion
    expected maximum depth
    final algorithm invariants
    frame-guard success
    final PASS state

  Run each again in normal display mode for visual inspection.

  Capture evidence frames only after each program's owned-half geometry,
  colours, labels, and final state match this runbook.

PHASE 7 - ZX-UX CONCURRENT PROOF - DEFERRED, SEPARATELY EXECUTABLE

  This phase is intentionally NOT part of the current SDK implementation,
  verification, acceptance, or release-completion criteria.

  ZX-UX is not yet ready for this concurrent proof.

  Do not execute this phase automatically after Phase 6.

  Execute Phase 7 only on a separate explicit request, after the native ZX-UX
  multitasking environment is declared ready for the test.

  When separately requested, launch both tracked programs as independent ZX-UX
  tasks using the native multitasking facilities.

  Start order must then be tested both ways:
    Hanoi first, then 8-Queens
    8-Queens first, then Hanoi

  During overlap verify:
    both halves continue to advance
    neither half is erased by the other task
    no UDG pattern changes unexpectedly
    no colour attributes bleed across x=128
    neither task changes the border
    both recursive displays survive scheduling
    both final algorithm results remain valid

  Repeat with forced or frequent task switches if the operating system test
  environment provides such a facility.

  Any visual corruption, wrong counter, wrong final board, wrong final tower,
  frame-guard failure, or non-zero process result is a failed multitasking
  proof.

  Completion of Phase 6 is sufficient for the present SDK work. Phase 7 remains
  a preserved future runbook procedure until separately requested.

9. SDK VERIFICATION REQUIREMENTS

The repository verification work added with the examples must prove more than
successful compilation.

For each source/binary pair:

  source must compile successfully
  output must match the tracked compiled artifact
  fast mode must run the full algorithm to completion
  process status must be zero
  internal invariants must all pass
  maximum recursion depth must match the frozen value
  frame guards must remain intact
  final state must match the frozen result

Hanoi additionally proves:
  127 legal moves
  all seven discs transferred A to C
  legal stack ordering after every move

8-Queens additionally proves:
  deterministic first solution
  eight unique columns
  conflict-free diagonals
  repeated recursive backtracking

The normal display runs are a second layer of proof and must not replace the
headless or fast algorithm checks.

Current SDK acceptance ends with Phase 6. The deferred concurrent ZX-UX proof
in Phase 7 is not a prerequisite for current SDK acceptance and must be run
only as a separately requested future procedure.

10. FAILURE POLICY

A program must fail closed.

On an internal invariant failure:

  stop making algorithmic progress
  keep all further drawing inside the owned half
  show FAIL and a short local reason
  return a non-zero status

Do not attempt to repair a corrupted logical model and continue.

Do not clear the whole screen after failure.

Do not overwrite the other program's half to make the failure more visible.

